---
import LegalPage from '../components/LegalPage.astro';

const repo = 'https://github.com/Lunatico0/lunatic-battery';
const codeSigningHref = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}code-signing/`;
---

<LegalPage title="Privacy policy" updated="2026-11-26">
  <p>
    This policy covers Lunatic Battery, a free, open source Windows tray app that shows the battery level of wireless
    peripherals, and this website. The app has no accounts, no telemetry, no analytics and no crash reporting service.
    The maintainer does not collect, receive or store any data about you or your devices.
  </p>

  <h2>What the app does on your PC</h2>
  <p>
    The app reads battery levels from your devices locally, through Windows (HID, Bluetooth and XInput). Those readings
    are shown in the tray and are never sent anywhere.
  </p>

  <h2>What the app sends over the network</h2>
  <p>The only thing the app does on the network by itself is check for updates:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      An installed copy contacts GitHub to look for a newer release of
      <a href={repo}>Lunatico0/lunatic-battery</a>: one minute after the app starts, then every 6 hours while it runs,
      and whenever you pick "Buscar actualizaciones" (Check for updates) in the tray menu. A copy run straight from a
      build folder does not check.
    </li>
    <li>
      These are normal HTTPS requests to GitHub. GitHub sees your IP address, as with any download. The app sends no
      personal data, device names, battery readings or settings.
    </li>
    <li>
      When a newer release exists, it is downloaded from GitHub in the background and installed when the app exits or
      restarts.
    </li>
    <li>
      There is no setting to turn the automatic check off. To stop it, block the app in your firewall: the battery
      display keeps working, it just stops updating.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Besides that, the app opens a web page only when you ask for it: the "Repositorio en GitHub" link in the settings
    window opens the project page on GitHub in your browser.
  </p>

  <h2>What the app keeps on your PC</h2>
  <p>Everything below stays on your computer. None of it is sent anywhere.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Settings: <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\LunaticBattery\settings.json</code>. It holds your preferences (tray icon mode,
      notifications, appearance) and, per device, the name you gave it, whether it is pinned or hidden, its low
      battery threshold and whether to notify you when it is charged.
    </li>
    <li>
      Diagnostic log: <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\LunaticBattery\logs\lunatic-battery.log</code>, with one older copy
      (<code>lunatic-battery.1.log</code>) once it reaches 1 MB. It records the app version, the Windows version, how
      the app was installed and the path it runs from, plus device discovery events and errors. You can open its folder
      from the tray menu ("Abrir registro").
    </li>
    <li>
      Start with Windows: if you turn it on, the app adds an entry to
      <code>HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run</code>. Uninstalling the app removes it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The diagnostic report</h2>
  <p>
    The "Mi dispositivo no aparece" window builds a report of what Windows sees of your devices (HID, Bluetooth and
    XInput), with the app and Windows versions. It leaves out device paths and serial numbers, and hides Bluetooth
    addresses. The report is built on your PC and is not sent anywhere: you can copy it or save it as a text file, and
    it only leaves your computer if you share it yourself, for example in a GitHub issue.
  </p>

  <h2>Third party services</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      GitHub: downloads, updates, source code and issues are hosted on GitHub. When you download the app, the app checks
      for updates, or you visit the repository, GitHub processes that request under the
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">
        GitHub General Privacy Statement</a
      >.
    </li>
    <li>
      GitHub Pages: this website is hosted on GitHub Pages, which may log your IP address when you visit it. The site
      has no cookies, no analytics and no tracking scripts.
    </li>
    <li>
      Google Fonts: this website loads its fonts from Google Fonts (<code>fonts.googleapis.com</code> and
      <code>fonts.gstatic.com</code>), so your browser sends Google your IP address when it loads a page. See the
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google Privacy Policy</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Code signing</h2>
  <p>
    How releases are built and signed is described in the <a href={codeSigningHref}>code signing policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about this policy go to the maintainer, Patricio Pittana, through
    <a href={`${repo}/issues`}>the project's GitHub issues</a>.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
